WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:01.807
Two teams want AI.

2
00:00:01.827 --> 00:00:03.580
Both need you first.

3
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.465
Merchandising wants a demand forecast.

4
00:00:06.485 --> 00:00:11.025
And the product team wants a shopping assistant that answers from the catalog.

5
00:00:11.045 --> 00:00:13.000
Your job is to get the data ready.

6
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.075
Three ideas.

7
00:00:15.095 --> 00:00:16.625
First: BigQuery ML.

8
00:00:16.645 --> 00:00:18.685
Machine learning, in SQL.

9
00:00:18.705 --> 00:00:21.760
You write create model, and train it right where the data lives.

10
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.270
No export.

11
00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:25.495
To predict a number, use regression.

12
00:00:25.515 --> 00:00:27.795
For yes or no, classification.

13
00:00:27.815 --> 00:00:30.480
For groups with no labels, k-means.

14
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:34.965
And for a forecast over time, like demand, use ARIMA PLUS.

15
00:00:34.985 --> 00:00:37.628
Put your clean-up steps in the transform clause.

16
00:00:37.648 --> 00:00:40.850
Then they run in training, and again at prediction.

17
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:45.600
The trap: exporting data to train a simple model somewhere else.

18
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.835
Second: a few basics the exam still asks.

19
00:00:48.855 --> 00:00:52.435
Overfitting is like a student who memorized last year's answers.

20
00:00:52.455 --> 00:00:55.820
Great in practice, lost in the real exam.

21
00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.655
The fixes: regularization, dropout, a simpler model, or more data.

22
00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:03.417
With rare cases, like fraud, accuracy lies.

23
00:01:03.437 --> 00:01:05.540
Use precision and recall.

24
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.890
When behaviour changes over time, retrain on old and new data together.

25
00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:11.990
Not only the new.

26
00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:13.665
Third: the shopping assistant.

27
00:01:13.685 --> 00:01:15.425
This is called RAG.

28
00:01:15.445 --> 00:01:17.588
The model does not know your catalog.

29
00:01:17.608 --> 00:01:20.685
So you look things up first, then let it answer.

30
00:01:20.705 --> 00:01:21.510
Five steps.

31
00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.335
Chunk the documents.

32
00:01:22.355 --> 00:01:23.285
Embed them.

33
00:01:23.305 --> 00:01:24.385
Index the vectors.

34
00:01:24.405 --> 00:01:25.222
Retrieve.

35
00:01:25.242 --> 00:01:26.670
Then generate.

36
00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:31.805
An embedding turns text into numbers, so that similar meanings sit close together.

37
00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:36.665
In BigQuery, you can embed, index, and run vector search, all in SQL.

38
00:01:36.685 --> 00:01:41.390
The trap: embedding questions with a different model than the documents.

39
00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:42.650
Recap.

40
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.575
Train in SQL with create model.

41
00:01:44.595 --> 00:01:45.800
Fight overfitting.

42
00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.535
And for RAG, use one embedding model.

43
00:01:48.555 --> 00:01:51.330
Try the checkpoint below.
